                "text": "**Analyzing the Visual Data**\n\nI've observed consistent vertical lines along the borders of the training examples. Specifically, the leftmost column consistently contains the digit \"8\", while the rightmost column shows the digit \"9\". I'm also noticing internal patterns within these examples, like the prevalence of \"4s\". Input 1's \"4\" is located in the row 1, column 3 position. I am looking for other pattern data in the training data.\n\n\n**Investigating the Mapping Logic**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on a critical clue! I realized why the digit at (1,3) transformed to \"0\". It seems the grid column position, relative to the left and right borders (\"8\" and \"9\" respectively), plays a role in the output. When it's centrally located, it seems to vanish. Now I'm checking other examples. I'm seeing that values closer to the leftmost \"8\" become \"8\", and values closer to the rightmost \"9\" become \"9\". I'm also finding an interesting exception in training data 3: input pixels of color \"4\" remain unchanged, while pixels of color \"3\" undergo this spatial-based transformation.\n\n\n**Deciphering the Transformations**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on a central clue, the column position relative to the grid borders! In train 2, values shift based on proximity to the border digits. The central digit vanishes because it's equidistant. The color that appears most? In train 3, pixels of color \"3\" undergo this spatial transformation, while \"4\"s remain static.\n\n\n**Unraveling Color Relationships**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on the importance of color. In each training set, the borders have unique colors: train 1 has teal and maroon; train 2 has red and orange; train 3 has blue and red. It seems the color of the \"points\" is important to note as well. I've noted that equidistant points may vanish. My attention is drawn to why the number of points in each training set may or may not cause a point to remain static.\n\n\n**Pinpointing the Key Feature**\n\nI'm now focusing on the role of the \"first pixel.\" In training set 3, I'm noting the significance of the number '3' and why it transforms, unlike '4'. I'm also examining the column data, where the first non-border pixel seems to influence the color transformation, or perhaps why the \"active\" color is '3'. I see patterns appearing! The first non-border pixel dictates the active transformation.\n\n\n**Determining the Active Color**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on the mechanism that selects the active color! I'm suspecting it's the most frequent non-background color in the interior. I've noted that 4 may sometimes be transformed, which challenges earlier hypotheses. Now I'm checking the distribution of pixels. I'm focusing on the first foreground colors appearing in each set, to see if there's a correlation.\n\n\n",
